@page
@model CommunityHub.Pages.Organizer.SoMeSchedulerModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Post planner";
}

<div class="card">
    <h1>Post planner</h1>
    <p class="muted">
        Plans the announcement campaign across the five post types and holds every post for your
        approval. <strong>It adds — it never rewrites.</strong> A post you have edited, approved or
        rescheduled is left alone on every later run.
    </p>

    @if (Model.AccessDenied)
    {
        <p class="error">@Localizer["Common.OrganizersOnly"]</p>
    }
    else
    {
        @if (Model.Message is not null)
        {
            <p class="@(Model.MessageIsError ? "error" : "success")">@Model.Message</p>
        }

        @* 🔑 §833.1 — THE REASON, ON SCREEN. This refusal used to exist only in a log, which made an
           engine that was working-as-designed look like an engine that was broken. *@
        @if (Model.EditionAlreadyStarted)
        {
            <p class="error">
                <strong>The planner is not composing.</strong> This edition has already started, and
                announcing an event that has begun is worse than not announcing it.
            </p>
        }
        else if (Model.MissingFooterParts.Count > 0)
        {
            <p class="error">
                <strong>The planner is composing nothing, on purpose.</strong>
                This edition has no
                <strong>@string.Join(", no ", Model.MissingFooterParts)</strong> yet.
            </p>
            <p class="muted">
                Posts are composed once and never rewritten, so planning now would queue them
                permanently without that part of the footer — and the gap closes so cleanly that the
                posts would look finished. Fill it in and the planner starts working immediately.
            </p>
            <a class="btn primary" asp-page="/Organizer/SoMeSettings">Fill in the post footer →</a>
        }
        else
        {
            <p class="success"><strong>Ready.</strong> The post footer is complete for this edition.</p>
        }

        <form method="post" asp-page-handler="Run" style="margin-top:12px;">
            <button type="submit" class="btn @(Model.CanCompose ? "primary" : "")">Plan posts now</button>
            <p class="muted" style="margin-top:8px;">
                Safe to run at any time: every post it creates is <strong>held (not approved)</strong>,
                so nothing can reach the company page until you turn it on in the queue.
            </p>
        </form>
    }
</div>

@if (!Model.AccessDenied)
{
    <div class="card">
        <h2>The campaign right now</h2>
        <p class="muted">
            @Model.TotalPlanned held for approval · @Model.TotalApproved approved and waiting ·
            @Model.TotalPublished published
        </p>

        <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
            <table>
                <caption class="sr-only">Planned, approved and published posts by type</caption>
                <tr style="text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid #ddd;">
                    <th scope="col">Post type</th>
                    <th scope="col">Held for approval</th>
                    <th scope="col">Approved</th>
                    <th scope="col">Published</th>
                    <th scope="col">Next one goes out</th>
                </tr>
                @foreach (var row in Model.Rows)
                {
                    <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee;">
                        <td>@row.Name</td>
                        <td>@(row.Planned > 0 ? row.Planned.ToString() : "—")</td>
                        <td>@(row.Approved > 0 ? row.Approved.ToString() : "—")</td>
                        <td>@(row.Published > 0 ? row.Published.ToString() : "—")</td>
                        <td class="muted">
                            @* §844.5 — Danish local time. *@
                            @(row.NextDue is null ? "—" : CommunityHub.Core.Integrations.SoMeDisplayTime.DateTime(row.NextDue.Value))
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </table>
        </div>

        @* 🔴 §918 — THE AUTO-APPROVAL RULES, WRITTEN WHERE HE WORKS. Operator 2026-08-06:
           *"document the auto-approval process in the menu for us to understand"*. A rule that
           publishes on your behalf has to be readable by the people it acts for — otherwise the
           only way to learn what it does is to watch what it did. *@
        <div class="card" style="margin-top:14px;background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">
            <h3 style="margin-top:0;">How auto-approval works</h3>
            <p class="muted">
                Posts are always <strong>planned automatically</strong> from the templates, and are
                always created <strong>held</strong> — nothing reaches LinkedIn until it is approved.
                Auto-approval does the approving for you, under three rules it will not break:
            </p>
            <ol class="muted" style="margin:0 0 8px 18px;">
                <li>
                    <strong>Only template-built posts.</strong> Types 1–4, untouched. Anything you
                    wrote or edited yourself — including every event post — still waits for you,
                    because those carry your words.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Only posts far enough ahead.</strong> A post must be at least the lead
                    time away before it can be approved. Anything sooner, or already overdue, is
                    left for you. <em>This is what stops a queue of past-dated posts publishing all
                    at once the moment approval is switched on.</em>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Only posts with nothing missing.</strong> The same readiness check you
                    see on a post — a sponsor still owing their logo or social text is never
                    auto-approved, and the reason is shown on their own page.
                </li>
            </ol>
            <p class="muted" style="margin-bottom:0;">
                The lead time doubles as your review window: an auto-approved post cannot publish for
                at least that many days, so there is always time to change or un-approve it. Turn it
                on, and set the lead time, in
                <a asp-page="/Organizer/SoMeSettings">SoMe settings</a>.
            </p>
        </div>

        @* 🔴 §1199 — WHERE "no room" GETS ITS ARITHMETIC. Operator 2026-09-12: *"we cannot sell the
           same seat twice like an airplane company"*. This page reports that a subject could not be
           placed; it never showed by how much, where in the year, or what would fix it. Linked from
           here rather than added as an eighth hub card — the answer belongs beside the failure. *@
        <p class="muted" style="margin-top:12px;">
            <a asp-page="/Organizer/SoMeCapacity"><strong>Posting capacity →</strong></a>
            how many slots exist between now and the event, how many are taken, and whether what is
            left to announce will fit.
        </p>

        <p class="muted" style="margin-top:12px;">
            Approve, edit or reschedule an individual post in the
            <a asp-page="/Organizer/SoMeQueue">Calendar for LinkedIn Posts</a>. Change the wording every post is built
            from in <a asp-page="/Organizer/SoMeTemplates">post templates</a>, and how OFTEN each
            type is announced in <a asp-page="/Organizer/SoMeSettings">SoMe settings</a>.
        </p>
        @* 🔒 §1207 — the old frequency page KEEPS ITS LINK even though the setting moved. The
           reachability guardrail is right: a page that still exists must stay reachable, and someone
           who remembers "posting frequency" needs to land somewhere that tells them where it went
           rather than on a 404. The page itself now says so and links onward. *@
        <p class="muted" style="margin-top:4px;">
            Looking for <a asp-page="/Organizer/SoMeCadence">posting frequency</a>? Rounds moved in
            with the rest of each category's rules.
        </p>
    </div>
}
